  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "After dodging death, battery startup Our Next Energy is now going public",
      "link": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/16/after-dodging-death-battery-startup-our-next-energy-is-now-going-public/",
      "description": "Our Next Energy is the latest battery startup to go public via SPAC. Does it now have what it needs to survive in the cutthroat battery industry?",
      "source_name": "TechCrunch",
      "published": "2026-11-16T13:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "Large Language Model-Empowered Intelligent Agent for Adaptive Fast-Charging Control of Lithium-Ion Batteries",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18140",
      "description": "arXiv:2611.18140v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Fast charging of lithium-ion batteries is a multi-objective control problem that requires reducing charging time and sustaining high energy throughput while suppressing accelerated degradation and safety-critical electrochemical reactions. Conventional protocols are easy to implement but have limited ability to adapt to variations in battery chemistry, operating conditions, and aging state. Model-based optimization and conventional data-driven control often require accurate models, costly iterative search, or task-specific policies. We develop a large language model (LLM)-empowered intelligent agent for adaptive battery fast-charging control. In this closed-loop framework, the agent interprets structured battery states, generates executable charging actions, observes the resulting battery response, and continuously adjusts subsequent decisions. We construct a large-scale simulation corpus with PyBaMM across five battery chemistries and 11 parameter sets and convert the resulting state-action-response trajectories into structured process data for agent training. Our two-stage strategy combines supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). SFT establishes battery-control semantics and feasible decision priors from simulation-derived demonstrations. GRPO aligns the agent with a multi-objective control objective covering energy throughput, degradation mitigation, and operational constraints. Closed-loop simulations show that, under matched energy-throughput requirements, the agent achieves longer cycling life than conventional charging baselines and optimization-based strategies across heterogeneous battery configurations. This study provides a simulation-grounded proof of concept for using LLM agents as sequential decision makers in battery management and takes a step toward language-model-enabled control of physical energy systems.",
      "source_name": "arXiv AI",
      "published": "2026-11-16T05:00:00"
    }
  ]
